{"conditionalSetIds":[],"forecasterId":"demographics-ai-2036:run1:inflation:inside","informationSetArtifactId":"sha256:ca33e06694a0a77b930dda0cb561f64a93325c0f7e5373be015f9ce9f684b16c","modelForecastIds":[],"modelUse":"none","prediction":{"kind":"categorical","probabilities":{"A":0.11,"B":0.44,"C":0.11,"D":0.34}},"questionHash":"sha256:ca2a4311940635fba34c40e7278721deb2f4716732e6011c94e6eb0554263e61","questionId":"demographics-ai-2036-cq6","questionVersion":"1.0.0","rationale":"Applied the lexical lock to my own CQ3 distribution (P10 0.7 / P50 1.6 / P90 2.7, implied sigma ~0.78 pp) and my own 2032-2036 OECD core path (~1.9% central, i.e. LOWER than my 2027-2036 average of 2.08% because the back half is the post-bust disinflation half).\nStep 1, A requires growth ≥2.6%: P = 1 - Phi((2.6-1.6)/0.78) = 1 - Phi(1.28) = 0.10; raised slightly to 0.11 for the right-tail case where no bust occurs and the migration channel relieves the care wall. A = 11.\nStep 2, C requires core ≥2.7% AND growth <2.0%: P(growth<2.0) = Phi(0.51) = 0.70. P(core ≥2.7 | growth<2.0) = 0.16 — conditional on low growth, high core must be SUPPLY-side, which in my mechanism means the care bottleneck clearing through prices instead of administered rationing, or a grid wall that survives the capex digestion, or outright fiscal dominance. My monopsony arrow is what holds this to 0.16 rather than 0.35. C = 0.70 x 0.16 = 0.11 -> 11.\nStep 3, D requires growth <1.7% AND core <2.0%: P(growth<1.7) = Phi(0.128) = 0.55. Within that cell: P(core≥2.7) = 0.17, P(core 2.0-2.7) = 0.21, P(core<2.0) = 0.62. D = 0.55 x 0.62 = 0.34.\nStep 4, B residual = 100 - 11 - 11 - 34 = 44.\nCONSISTENCY CHECK PERFORMED. (i) My CQ3 P50 of 1.6% sits just inside D's growth band, so D could not be small — but D also needs core <2.0%, which costs it 38% of that mass, so D lands at 34 and not ~55. (ii) B exceeds D (44 > 34) because B is the residual that absorbs BOTH the 1.7-2.5% growth band (~29% of mass) and the low-growth / 2.0-2.7% core leftover cell (~12%); it is not a \"muddle through\" judgement, it is an accounting residual of my own CQ3/core joint. (iii) Against CQ2 = 18: CQ2's inflation conjunct (core ≥2.50% averaged over 2027-2036) has marginal 0.30, which is deliberately HIGHER than C's requirement (core ≥2.7% over 2032-2036, marginal ~0.17) — the two are consistent only because my path is front-loaded, the high-inflation years being 2027-2029 and not the CQ6 window. If I had assumed a flat path these two numbers would conflict, and that front-loading is the single thing a reader should check me on. (iv) A = 11 cannot exceed my P(growth≥2.6) = 0.10-0.11, satisfied. (v) D + C = 45, i.e. I put slightly less than half the mass on the two \"something is broken\" regimes and the modal single regime on the bifurcated residual — the distinguishable claim is that D at 34 is roughly 3x the 2B standalone ensemble's implied stagnation weight, because endogenous demographics add the care-rationing GDP leak and the BIS Box C demand deficit that the exogenous-demographics run cannot see.","referenceClassIds":[],"schemaVersion":"forecast-workbench.forecast/v1","sealedAt":"2026-11-04T17:31:52.250Z","seriesId":"published-panel","strongestContraryCase":"Not elicited per question. Panelist falsification signposts: {\"signpost\":\"OECD-38 GDP-weighted health-and-social-care services price index (or, as a readable proxy, the US CPI medical-care-services index together with euro-area HICP health services) runs at or above 4.5% year-on-year for four consecutive quarters, with public-sector pay settlements in ISIC Q at or above 4%/yr in at least five of the G7.\",\"by_when\":\"Dec 31, 2028\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"Falsifies my central monopsony arrow — the care bottleneck would be clearing through PRICES, not administered rationing. Core level path rises ~+0.35 pp, so CQ2 goes 18 -> 30 (first conjunct marginal 0.30 -> 0.48); CQ6-C +10 (11 -> 21) and CQ6-D -12 (34 -> 22); CQ1 rises 22 -> 31 via conjunct 2; CQ3 P50 rises ~+0.15 pp because the rationing GDP leak and the informal-carer labour-force exit shrink.\"} | {\"signpost\":\"Cumulative announced-then-cancelled-or-impaired hyperscaler AI capex stays below 10% of announced buildouts AND net external AI software/API revenue exceeds 1.0x annualized AI compute D&A for four consecutive quarters (i.e. locked AI-Q4 is resolving NO).\",\"by_when\":\"Jun 30, 2030\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"Removes the 2031-2033 bust from the middle of every 2032-2036 measurement window. CQ3 P50 1.6 -> 2.1, P90 2.7 -> 3.2; CQ4 P50 1.8 -> 2.2 (investment demand holds r* up); CQ6-A 11 -> 22 and CQ6-D 34 -> 18; CQ5 falls 34 -> 26 as the leveraged-unwind shock source thins. This is the single highest-leverage signpost for my numbers.\"} | {\"signpost\":\"China CPI 12-month average at or above 2.0% in two consecutive calendar years, together with the household income share of GDP printing above 47% (or household consumption above 45% of GDP) in the national accounts.\",\"by_when\":\"Dec 31, 2031\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"Falsifies the China-as-independent-deflation-object mechanism. CQ2 falls 18 -> 9 (second conjunct 0.70 -> 0.30, and the sign of the inter-conjunct correlation flips to positive). Simultaneously weakens the OECD core-goods disinflation channel, so OECD core rises ~+0.15 pp: CQ6-C +4, CQ6-D -5, and my CQ3 China leg rises to ~3.4%, lifting the combined P50 ~+0.15.\"} | {\"signpost\":\"US average retail electricity price per kWh (BLS/EIA) deflated by core CPI rises less than 8% cumulatively from calendar 2026, while PJM and ERCOT installed reserve margins both sit above 18% and interconnection queue duration for firm capacity falls below 3 years.\",\"by_when\":\"Dec 31, 2030\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"Falsifies the tight-capacity convexity arrow (residual-demand price semi-elasticity 2.5 on a sub-15% reserve margin) and the grid-wall leg of the relative-price skew. The 2027-2030 impulse loses ~0.12 pp of core and the skew compresses toward a uniform low-inflation regime: CQ6-C 11 -> 6, CQ6-D 34 -> 38, CQ2 18 -> 13. It would also mean the locked AI-Q3 load path is physically buildable, partially retiring my coupling tension.\"} | {\"signpost\":\"At the two-thirds mark of the CQ1 window, OECD-38 ISIC F+Q real median hourly wage cumulative growth since 2025 exceeds ISIC J+K+M by 7.0 percentage points or more (OECD/Eurostat SES and US OES, composition-unadjusted, i.e. on the resolution definition).\",\"by_when\":\"Dec 31, 2033\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"Direct read on CQ1's binding conjunct: a gap of 7 pp or more implies the compositional drags are weaker than my -0.3/-0.5 pp/yr estimate and CQ1 goes 22 -> 34; a gap under 3.0 pp implies the migrant-entrant and bottom-displacement composition effects dominate and CQ1 goes 22 -> 12, with CQ6-B gaining ~+4 from C at the same time.\"}","updateBasis":{"kind":"launch","note":"run1 wave 1 inside seat, effort 'inflation'."}}